{"kind":"request","text":"The README sells ticket numbers as the first of three rungs, and nothing exposes one. Hugh raised this after watching me fabricate event identifiers three times in one session; it is the systemic cause rather than a nicety.\n\nREADME.md:69-72 states the claim plainly: \"Every event gets a ticket number. Signed at the door, final, the same number for every reader. 'As of #4312' means one exact thing, forever.\" Rungs two and three are written in the same vocabulary — \"Rests on #4290\", \"Replaces #4101\", \"Projected from #4312\".\n\nNo such number is exposed anywhere. A projected statement carries event, actor, kind, text, body and timestamp, and `event` is the full `git:sha1:\u003cgenesis\u003e#git:sha1:\u003c40-hex\u003e`. There is no number, index or ordinal in the projection, in `gs status --json`, or in any MCP tool response. SKILL.md:165 then instructs agents the other way: \"Always use the canonical full event ID.\"\n\nThe position already exists. internal/workroom/fold.go carries an `index` per parsed record — it is how the founding-seed rule works, `record.index == 0`. This is surfacing something the fold already computes, not inventing a concept.\n\nWhy it is worth doing beyond matching the README. A 40-hex identifier has no redundancy: every character is equally plausible, so a wrong one looks exactly like a right one until the fold rejects it. All three of my failures today were the same shape — read an eight-character prefix from a listing, write a plausible remainder — and each produced an act that appended cleanly, reported success, and carried no force. One cost a completed review twenty minutes of being invisible; one would have put an unresolvable citation into an immutable commit trailer. A short ordinal is checkable by eye against a listing and cannot be confabulated in the same way, because inventing one is noticeable.\n\nTwo constraints for whoever takes this, because getting them wrong would make things worse. The number is meaningful only within one genesis, so anything crossing workrooms still needs the canonical form and the display must not imply otherwise. And it should be a display and lookup affordance, not a second identifier the fold accepts as a citation — two names for one thing is its own trap, and the fold's refusal to resolve a wrong identifier is a property worth keeping.","body":{"conditions":"Every projected event carries its sequence position, exposed through the projection and the MCP surface, so a reader and an agent can name an event as `#N` within a workroom; the number is derived from the fold's existing per-record position rather than newly assigned, and is stable for a given genesis and event; the documentation and SKILL.md agree with the implementation — either agents are told to cite `#N` where a workroom is unambiguous, or the README's ticket-number claim is corrected to match what exists, and whichever is chosen is recorded with its reason; cross-workroom citation still requires the canonical full identifier and the surfaces make that boundary visible rather than implicit; the fold's citation resolution is unchanged unless changing it is a deliberate decision recorded separately, since a second accepted name for one event is a trap of its own; a test pins the number's stability across a re-fold of the same log; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}